# Spiking and Sounding Neural Network (SSNN) Implementation Plan

SSNN is an advanced, high-fidelity Spiking Neural Network synthesizer feature. It models a leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) neural network containing 960 neurons across 32 layers running alongside the Web Audio synthesis engine. It listens, learns (via continuous FFT analysis mapped to connection weights), and resynthesizes its own input using eight synthesis engines: Pulse, Modal, SYNaptic, Granular, FM, Comb, Tape, and Arpeg.

This plan details the design, architecture, components, and phase-by-phase task list for developing and integrating the SSNN synthesizer into Aether Sonic.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Production Choice - High-Performance Web Worker Architecture:**
> To ensure flawless performance and zero build/deployment issues for a live internet launch, we will implement the 960-neuron LIF network simulation using highly optimized TypeScript and **Web Workers** rather than WebAssembly (C++/Rust). 
> * **Zero Build Risk:** Does not require local C++/Rust compiler setups or emscripten binaries on deployment platforms (Vercel, Netlify).
> * **Zero Main-Thread Lag:** The spiking calculation runs in a background thread, preventing visual lag or Web Audio frame drops.
> * **Garbage-Collector-Free:** Stores potentials, connections, and weight matrices in flat `Float32Array` buffers.


> [!TIP]
> The Pulse generator will simulate electronic component sounds (resistors, capacitors, relays, transistors, 555 timers) by utilizing low-level noise filters, envelope triggers, and pulse generators.

